George always appreciate your time I  guess I'll just start with what's the  NFLPA official position on this letter  from Adolpho birch in the NFL good  morning to you Ross and I wish we were  both at Copacabana beach with Dan at the  moment but you know certainly we were a  little bit taken aback with the tone of  that letter certainly there are areas  that we can choose to disagree with the  league we have a lot of respect for the  game our players have a lot of respect  for what they do as professionals but  you know I think that that letter in  particular cross the line of what is  professional conduct as it relates to  dealing with with your players and you  know we've always taken the position  that the NFL needs to step up and  provide any evidence that they have  beyond just a report that has now been  recanted or was immediately recanted and  they haven't done that yet so for them  to make a mountain out of this mole hill  is unfortunate and it's disappointing  but ultimately it's our job to represent  our players and that's what we'll do so  George's for the people that are just  happening on this thing basically the  NFLPA agrees that if there's credible  evidence in terms of a violation then  their players should sit for the  interview you're saying and the players  have contended that there is not  credible evidence whereas the league is  saying in their opinion there is  credible evidence is that really what  this is coming down to uh I think that's  a pretty good explanation so when the  report first came out I guess it was in  December there were all these names  dropped in that report and you know the  most notable one obviously was Payton  inning these four players were named in  some way by general  names Charlie sly as soon as that report  was public he came out and recanted  everything and the NFL has contended  that based on that soul piece of  evidence as far as we know that the  players need to submit to not only an  investigation but an interview and all  sorts of other things we don't believe  that that is outlined in the CBA now  ultimately when you are in a position  like we are now we're going to support  the players and whatever they decide to  do they can it's always been their  choice as to whether or not to proceed  with the interviews or not the union is  going to represent them if they decide  to move forward the union will be there  if they decide to not sit down for the  interview the union will be there for  them as well but what advice are you  giving them George well I mean like  everything else so in any other  situation that we've gone through over  the last six or seven years these are  your rights these are the decisions that  you have to choose from and these are  the consequences for each and risks for  each of those decisions so again we've  talked about the rights we don't believe  that the league has a right to impose  you know the players coming in for an  interview based on nothing that is clear  we certainly don't think they have any  right to impose discipline based on  what's out there there is nothing out  there but obviously the risks to and the  consequences to them denying that if you  take the league at their word is that  they'll be suspended indefinitely in  miss games which will obviously lead to  another fight of some sort down the road  we don't want to be in that position I  certainly hope the league doesn't want  to be in that position it's  disappointing that we're on the eve of  another great NFL season and the  headlines are not about what's happening  on the field but what's happening off  the  is the nflpa and the NFL in talks to try  to work something out here that's a  great question we would love to have  been in discussions to try to have this  thing resolved months ago that has not  been the case it look Ross this is not  our first go-around with this regime in  the league office they did the same  exact thing if you remember in bounty  they did the same exact thing when they  violated the CBA with Ray Rice now  clearly they happen to have gotten a  couple of court cases go in their favor  recently but that doesn't mean that the  law is a good substitute for  professionalism and a level of behavior  that is deemed you know to respect the  players of the National Football League  that's just who they are this is what  the job is and and we will stand up for  our players no matter what George  refresh my memory has there been players  suspended for not cooperating or not  talk I know far I've got you know fine  50 grand or something for not  cooperating is there any precedent for  guys to be suspended and miss football  games for not agreeing to talk to the  league no there is no precedent for such  a thing and you know Saints fans know  all too well what happened to their  franchise obviously Patriots fans are  still mad and I think will always be mad  about you know the fact that tom is  going to miss the first four games of  this season and and now Packers fans and  steeler fans are getting a taste of you  know when you have a league office that  doesn't kind of respect the rules of  engagement this is what happens so there  isn't there isn't a precedent for that  we certainly don't think that this is  the the right thing to do but you know  we  we will do what we have to do given what  the players decide here in the next  couple of days talking with George  Atallah the NFLPA assistant executive  director of external affairs here on the  Dan Patrick show George based on the NFL  exonerating Peyton Manning they clearly  are saying that they don't believe  Charlie sly to be credible yet they  still want to interview these guys so  are they just doing this because they  can I mean is this just kind of a power  play you know let's say they have two  interviewer will suspend them because we  can because they can't be that concerned  about these guys actually taking peds  because they just exonerated Payton yeah  that's a great point I mean that is that  is what logic and reason would dictate  that you have no evidence you have not  produced any evidence and you've just  exonerated Payton for the you know  arguably who was a more notable subject  of the documentary than any of the four  players that we're talking about so  logic and reason does not usually  dictate the behavior of the league  office oftentimes you know again we  would much rather be in a world where  we're talking about issues that are  about to happen on the field you know as  much as people don't like the preseason  there's a lot to like about young  players getting a chance and teams  trying to rebuild and all of the things  that you want to talk about heading into  the football season this is not high on  our list of things to discuss but here  we are so two questions if the players  decide they're not going to interview I  guess number one would be what is the  what does the nflpa do then do you file  some sort of legal injunction or  something that I'm glad I didn't go to  law school for or do you pay the players  money do you pay the players money that  are missing out on if they're suspended  well there are certainly legal options  if the players decide that they do not  want to sit down for the interview you  know Ross you and I have talked to know  x I'm not a lawyer we have a very  capable internal and external legal  counsel who whose job it is to worry  about those things you know I think  there are certainly recourses that we  can take to protect their rights if they  don't want to do that again our advice  to the players has always been these are  your rights know what they are you do  not have to sit down for this because  the drug policy do not call for a  submission of interviews if there isn't  any credible evidence and if you decide  to go through with it great if you  decide not to we're always up for a  fight last question George and this  one's totally off topic but we're going  to talk about Marcell Dareus a little  bit and his suspension does the NFLPA  have an official position on marijuana  testing in the league yeah the official  position is that we have a collectively  bargained drug policy that still  maintains that the banned substance  certainly we made some progressive  changes in 2014 when we sign the new  drug policies that you know elevated the  number of nanograms and offenses that  you needed in order to get suspended but  our focus as a union is more on the  medical usage in the scientific usage  going forward how can we treat that  substance I think we might have just  lost in that area yeah I said how can we  treat that substance as potentially one  that that deals with the pain management  issue that our players go through I mean  I think that's where our union is going  to be from this point forward but every  player knows that they have to adhere to  to the program pretty strictly and you  know that's that's where we are George  really appreciate the time thanks for  coming aboard this morning my pleasure  and hope to talk to you soon there he is  George Atallah the day  Patrick show weekday mornings on  audience
 

